hadiths[1]{hadithnumber,text}:
"305","Ahmad ibn Nasr al-Muqri, and Abdullah ibn al-Sabah al-Attar al-Basri, Ahmad said: He informed us, and Abdullah said: He informed us. Mahboub bin Al-Hassan, from Dawud, meaning Ibn Abi Hind, on the authority of Al-Sha’bi, on the authority of Masruq, on the authority of Aisha, who said: “The obligatory prayer when traveling and at home Two rak'ahs, two rak'ahs. When the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, stayed in Medina, two rak'ahs were added to the urban prayer, and the prayer was left Fajr prayer because it is a long recitation, and the Maghrib prayer because it is the odd number of the day.” Abu Bakr said: This is a strange hadith. It was not narrated by anyone I know of except Mahbub bin Al-Hasan. The companions of Dawud narrated it, and they said: On the authority of Al-Shaabi, on the authority of Aisha, that Mahboub bin Al-Hassan left behind"
